„­bible_version£KJV¤book§chapter¨sections•…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÈRighteous art thou, O LORD, when I plead with thee: yet let me talk with thee of thy judgments: Wherefore doth the way of the wicked prosper? wherefore are all they happy that deal very treacherously?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ’Thou hast planted them, yea, they have taken root: they grow, yea, they bring forth fruit: thou art near in their mouth, and far from their reins.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ©But thou, O LORD, knowest me: thou hast seen me, and tried mine heart toward thee: pull them out like sheep for the slaughter, and prepare them for the day of slaughter.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÌHow long shall the land mourn, and the herbs of every field wither, for the wickedness of them that dwell therein? the beasts are consumed, and the birds; because they said, He shall not see our last end.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙàIf thou hast run with the footmen, and they have wearied thee, then how canst thou contend with horses? and if in the land of peace, wherein thou trustedst, they wearied thee, then how wilt thou do in the swelling of Jordan?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÉFor even thy brethren, and the house of thy father, even they have dealt treacherously with thee; yea, they have called a multitude after thee: believe them not, though they speak fair words unto thee.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse	¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙI have forsaken mine house, I have left mine heritage; I have given the dearly beloved of my soul into the hand of her enemies.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙfMine heritage is unto me as a lion in the forest; it crieth out against me: therefore have I hated it.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse	¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ’Mine heritage is unto me as a speckled bird, the birds round about are against her; come ye, assemble all the beasts of the field, come to devour.¦layout ªlast_verse	…¤book§chapter«first_verse
